{{Short description|Financial crisis in Lacashe following the end of refugee admissions}}

The '''2026 Lacashian bond crisis''' is an ongoing financial crisis in [[Lacashe]] that began after the repeal of the [[Wolffrea War Refugee Act]] on 15 August 2026. The repeal ended both group-based refugee admissions and the quarterly hosting grants paid by the [[Adonian Community]]. The government retained housing, service, subsidy and lease obligations associated with the 712,431 admissions while holding I$3.7 billion in [[International Dollar|International-dollar]] sovereign bonds and obligations associated with [[Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates]].

Lacashe had issued foreign bonds from 2023 while AC grants and rising reserves improved its perceived capacity to service debt. The government used bond proceeds to expand construction, imports, utility subsidies and rural public employment beyond the expenditure financed by the grants. In 2025, it also sold securities backed by future rent from state-owned refugee farmland. Investors treated the AC transfers as a continuing source of foreign currency, although the grants were legally tied to new admissions and did not guarantee Lacashian debt.

After admissions ended, sovereign bond prices fell by between 25 and 40 per cent and secondary-market yields rose into the 13–16 per cent range. Settlement rent certificates lost between 70 and 80 per cent of their value. The hanti depreciated, foreign reserves declined and no further foreign bond issue was completed.

== Background ==

The government of President [[Markos Gurshaan]] supported the Wolffrea War Refugee Act partly as a population and labour policy. The AC paid I$7,850 for each refugee registered under the act, producing I$5.593 billion in transfers between 2022 and August 2026. Annual payments rose from I$64.5 million in 2022 to I$2.222 billion in 2025.

The grants financed the [[Wolffrean refugee settlement programme]], including trailer housing, roads, utilities, schools, clinics and agricultural support. They also supported imports of essential goods and construction materials and allowed the government to subsidize utility charges. Expenditure associated with the programme was estimated at I$6.902 billion for 2022–2026 on a full-year 2026 basis, exceeding the grants by approximately I$1.309 billion.

The government expected future tax revenue and rent from state agricultural leases to offset part of the difference. Refugee leases carried five-year rent holidays, however, and rent from the existing allocation cohorts would not begin until 2029–2030. Continued admissions were expected to produce both additional AC payments and a larger future rent base in later years.

== Foreign bond issuance ==

Lacashe issued bonds denominated in International dollars through syndicates of domestic and foreign banks. The AC transfers increased reserves and reduced the immediate foreign-exchange risk perceived by investors. The bonds were not guaranteed by the Adonian Community.

