The proceeds were deposited in International dollars and spent over time on imports, construction and general government programmes. Foreign reserves rose from I$3.2 billion in 2020 to an estimated I$6.25 billion in 2025. The availability of foreign financing permitted the government to maintain subsidies and construction despite a widening current-account deficit.

Bond servicing created a currency mismatch. Government revenue was collected principally in hanti, while interest and principal were payable in International dollars. Depreciation therefore increased the domestic-currency cost of debt even before refinancing.

== Settlement rent certificate market ==
{{main|Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates}}

In 2025 the Ministry of Finance sold I$1.25 billion in settlement rent certificates, commonly called rent futures. The securities transferred part of the future rent from state-owned refugee plots to investors. Their valuations used projections of continued refugee admissions and an expanding lease base.

The certificates reached a secondary-market value of approximately I$1.7 billion in early 2026. Banks and investment funds treated the securities as closely associated with the sovereign because the land remained state-owned and the government administered rent collection. Their legal documents did not contain a general state guarantee.

== Repeal of the refugee programme ==

The National Assembly passed the Refugee Adjustment Act by 118 votes to 2 on 15 August 2026. The act ended group-based admissions, transferred pending applications to the ordinary asylum procedure and dissolved the Office for Wolffrean Resettlement. Existing refugee rights and land leases were preserved.

The final AC grant was calculated from admissions recorded through 15 August. No grant accrued in the fourth quarter. Investors had expected future payments at a rate comparable to the I$2.222 billion transferred in 2025 and continued growth in the number of future rent-paying leases. The repeal removed both expectations while leaving existing programme expenditure in place.

The government had issued I$1.0 billion in bonds during the first half of 2026. The proximity of the issue to the repeal produced demands for an inquiry into the fiscal projections provided to investors. The bonds did not contain a promise that admissions or AC transfers would continue.

== Market collapse ==

Selling began after the repeal vote. Prices of Lacashian sovereign bonds declined by between 25 and 40 per cent during the following weeks. Yields on longer-dated issues rose into a range of approximately 13–16 per cent, preventing the government from completing further planned issuance on acceptable terms.

Settlement rent certificates experienced larger losses. Their prices fell by between 70 and 80 per cent because their projected rent base no longer included future admissions, most existing leases remained within their rent-free period, and the relocation provisions of the Refugee Adjustment Act created uncertainty over future collection. The fall in the hanti reduced the International-dollar value of rent assessed in domestic currency.

Foreign investors sold Lacashian bonds, certificates and bank deposits. The hanti, which averaged approximately 11.8 per International dollar in 2025, averaged 13.9 during 2026 and traded near 15.4 in November. Reserves declined from I$6.25 billion in 2025 to approximately I$4.1 billion by November 2026.

== Banking and economic effects ==

Domestic banks held sovereign bonds and settlement rent certificates as liquid assets and collateral. Falling prices reduced their capital positions and led them to restrict lending, particularly to construction companies and rural suppliers. Foreign banks increased collateral requirements for Lacashian counterparties.

Private construction had already been affected by labour and material shortages. The withdrawal of credit caused projects in Guledga and the coastal districts to be delayed or cancelled. Contractors in the settlement districts faced the end of new public tenders after the halt to admissions. Workers who had moved from cities to the rural construction sites began returning or entered temporary unemployment.

The depreciation of the hanti raised the cost of imported food, fuel and construction materials. Government subsidies limited immediate increases in administered prices but added to the fiscal deficit. Public debt was estimated at 54.8 per cent of GDP in 2026, compared with 45 per cent in 2020, and the budget deficit rose to an estimated 7.2 per cent of GDP.

== Regional financial contagion and Abyalan selloff ==

The collapse of Lacashian public credit quickly transmitted beyond domestic borders, triggering the broader [[2026 Abyalan bond market selloff]]. The primary transmission channel was the indexing and packaging of Lacashian sovereign bonds and Settlement Rent Certificates within actively managed exchange-traded funds operated by [[Bitllet per a Abyala|Grup BPA]] and listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]], most prominently the [[BPA Abyala Bond Fund]] (ABF) and the [[BPA Emerging Markets Bond Fund]] (EMBF). As panicked unitholders initiated substantial redemptions following the passage of the Refugee Adjustment Act, fund managers found secondary trading in the Settlement Rent Certificates frozen due to the total withdrawal of two-way quotations by market-makers such as [[Empresa per a Bons, Préstecs i Reemborsaments]] (EBPR). To fulfill contractual cash redemptions, the funds liquidated large holdings of liquid sovereign debt from [[Balboa]], [[Potocsí]], [[Tapuya]], and [[Asikyira]], depressing benchmark bond prices across the continent by 5% to 10% between late August and October 2026.

The contagion extended internationally to institutional investors in [[Okaiken]], where commercial trust banks, life insurance companies, and corporate pension funds had acquired large stakes in BPA fund units to escape domestic negative interest rates. The sharp contraction in net asset values forced Sanese institutions to record substantial mark-to-market write-downs, prompting the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] to launch regulatory inspections under the [[Banking Transparency Act]] of 2024 and resulting in executive shakeups in Okami.

With domestic foreign exchange reserves falling from I$6.25 billion to approximately I$4.1 billion by November 2026 at an unsustainable monthly drain of nearly I$700 million, Lacashe was shut out of voluntary private debt markets. Facing an impending balance-of-payments crisis, the government initiated preliminary discussions with the [[Adonian Community Monetary Facility]] (ACMF) to negotiate an emergency sovereign bailout. Market analysts and multilateral observers anticipated that any ACMF package would mandate severe fiscal consolidation, a sovereign debt restructuring imposing 40% to 50% haircuts on foreign bondholders, and the complete cancellation of the unbacked Settlement Rent Certificates.

